{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ונסלח", :translit "ve-nislach", :gloss "and he shall be forgiven"} {:he "וכי תבואו אל הארץ", :translit "u-chi tavo'u el ha-aretz", :gloss "and when you come into the land"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "smikhut parshiyot — juxtaposition of adjacent passages yields the inference"}], :frame {:text "The principle of semikhut ha-parshiyot treats the Torah's juxtaposition of two passages as carrying legal or theological significance. Here, the placement of Leviticus 19:23 ('And when you come into the land') immediately after Leviticus 19:22 ('and he shall be forgiven') yields the inference that residing in the Land of Israel itself effects atonement. A related idea is expressed in the Talmud: 'You have no finer dwelling place than the Land of Israel' (Ketubot 75a), and the Talmud further teaches that dwelling in the Land of Israel atones for sin, deriving from the verse 'and dwell in the land... and be nourished by faith' (Psalms 37:3) as discussed at Ketubot 111a.", :sources ["Ketubot 75a" "Ketubot 111a" "Leviticus 19:23" "Psalms 37:3"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "smikhut parshiyot — juxtaposition of adjacent passages yields the inference", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:24:23.891249138Z", :model "glm-5.2"}